{"kind":"request","text":"R3b's smallest alternative for b4a6626d, before a policy edit.\n\nThe old active read in the eviction witness starts before key0 has a denied record. Comparing only denied.get(key) before and after await therefore misses the later denial and its eviction. I propose temporary pending cells for that gap, with record identity for returned denial handles. No permanent per-key stamp or evicted-key record remains.\n\nPatch outline:\n1. In authority.ts, replace the run-wide generation with a Map\u003cKeyId, cell\u003e containing only outstanding raw Membership reads. A read acquires the current cell for its key and increments its reader count before await. Concurrent reads of the same key share it. Denied FIFO eviction deletes only the evicted key's cell. An older response for that key then fails the identity check. An admitted key's handles do not depend on this FIFO cell.\n2. Check cell identity immediately after the bounded await, before releasing the cell. Release in finally. The last outstanding reader removes the same cell; it must not delete a newer replacement cell. Capture the current denied Read identity for a returned denial handle. Its held/sealed checks require that identity while an admitted key stays outside FIFO cancellation. Preserve definite unknown-key denial and all existing grant/clock/head judgments.\n3. Bound the raw observe await inside authority.read with the existing within/LATE helper, using the existing seconds argument. namespace.ts membershipIn().observe ignores seconds and awaits peer.observe indefinitely. Scope.#standing's outer within is a Promise.race that returns without cancelling authority.read. A finally around that raw promise alone can therefore retain cells forever.\n4. Import within/LATE from turn.ts. The core/turn live-binding cycle already exists, and authority reads these bindings only when read runs after module initialization. No helper move or new deadline implementation is needed.\n\nAt inner expiry, treat the read as unanswered, release its pending cell, and ignore any eventual late response. n still increments once before the read; began, the on-time observation, and every current-standing judgment stay in their existing order. No timeout value increases. This deliberately changes an incidental old effect: after the outer caller timed out, a late answer could still update caches or reserve an admitted-key place. Retaining that effect after forgetting the cell would let an evicted key's old active answer escape the identity check. The new path does no post-timeout cache/admission work.\n\nTwo existing witness assertions must change. Admitted key5000's handle stays current. A fresh key0 read that inserts its denial and evicts key1 returns an ordinary empty denial [], because key0 was not evicted. The older key0 active read still returns null after key0's own eviction. Keeping the fresh key0 result null would retain the unrelated-key cancellation that b4a6626d forbids.\n\nChanged paths: packages/scope/src/authority.ts; packages/scope/test/authority.test.ts for those two assertions; docs/security/threat-model.md; and the R3b implementation note. The own-store module, one Node owner, one Native flood witness, and unbounded-insertion control keep their accepted scope. Regenerate the authority seal after the final source edit. No source policy, seal, or executable validation has changed for this proposal.\n\nPlease accept the temporary pending-cell lifetime, bounded raw read and explicit late-result change, and both assertion corrections before I apply them. Also clarify the retained R3a eviction-control obligation: its old fault removes generation++, which this version deletes. The equivalent identity fault removes the evicted-key cell cancellation. I will run no extra control until its place beside R3b's one unbounded-insertion control is clear.\n","body":{"conditions":"Rule on the attached per-key cancellation design before implementation.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}